   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend chapter 81 of the laws of 1995, amending the vehicle and\ntraffic law and other laws relating to the enforcement of support through the\nsuspension of driving privileges, in relation to the effectiveness thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S6388",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6388-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §246, Chap 81 of 1995",
    "lawSection": "Vehicle and Traffic",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6388\n\nSPONSOR: PERSAUD\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend chapter 81 of the laws of 1995, amend-\ning the vehicle and traffic law and other laws relating to the enforce-\nment of support through the suspension of driving privileges, in\nrelation to the effectiveness thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE OF THE BILL:\n\nThis bill would extend, for two years, the expiration of the driver\nlicense suspension process, one of the State's most effective tools to\nenforce child support obligations.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill would amend subdivision 19 of § 246 of Chapter 81\nof the Laws of 1995 to extend the administrative driver license suspen-\nsion process from August 31, 2019 to August 31, 2021.\n\nSection 2 of the bill would provide for an immediate effective date and\nwould be deemed to have been in full force and effect on and after\nAugust 31, 2019.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis bill would extend, for two years, the driver license suspension\nprocess, a highly successful enforcement process to collect child\nsupport obligations. Using this process, the child support program is\nable to: (1) identify a parent who fails to pay child support and who\nalso has a driver license; and (2) notify the parent that his or her\ndriver license will be suspended unless child support payments are paid.\n\nFederal law requires that each State must have a driver license suspen-\nsion process to improve the effectiveness of child support enforcement\n(42 United States Code § 666  \nA \n16)). NEW YORK'S ADMINISTRATIVE PROCESS\nSATISFIES THIS FEDERAL REQUIREMENT AND ACCOUNTS FOR SUBSTANTIAL\nCOLLECTIONS EACH YEAR. THE EXPIRATION OF THIS ENFORCEMENT TOOL WOULD\nHAVE A CONSIDERABLE IMPACT ON NEW YORK FAMILIES.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nTHIS PROVISION WAS LAST EXTENDED BY CHAPTER 138 OF THE LAWS OF 2017 FROM\nAUGUST 31, 2017 TO AUGUST 31, 2019.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nFAILING TO EXTEND THE DRIVER LICENSE SUSPENSION PROCESS WOULD RESULT IN\nTHE LOSS OF A POWERFUL DETERRENT FOR THE NON-PAYMENT OF CHILD SUPPORT\nOBLIGATIONS. THE DRIVER LICENSE SUSPENSION PROCESS ACCOUNTS FOR APPROXI-\nMATELY $10 MILLION IN CHILD SUPPORT COLLECTIONS ANNUALLY, WITH APPROXI-\nMATELY 95 PERCENT OF THE COLLECTIONS GOING TO FAMILIES.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nTHE BILL PROVIDES FOR AN IMMEDIATE EFFECTIVE DATE AND SHALL BE DEEMED TO\nHAVE BEEN IN FULL FORCE AND EFFECT ON AND AFTER AUGUST 31, 2019.\n",
